Implement a C program that suspends the system clock for a specific time duration.

A system clock is a device that keeps track of the current date and time. When the clock is suspended, the date and time remain unchanged until it is resumed. This functionality can be useful in various scenarios, such as testing time-dependent functionality, simulating past or future dates, and creating reproducible test environments.

To implement this program, you will need to:

1. Create a C program that accepts a duration in milliseconds as an argument.
2. Suspend the system clock for the specified duration.
3. Resume the system clock after the duration has elapsed.

Here is an example implementation in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: suspend_clock <duration_in_milliseconds>\n");
        return 1;
    }

    long duration = atol(argv[1]);

    printf("Suspending system clock for %ld milliseconds...\n", duration);
    sleep(duration / 1000);
    printf("System clock resumed.\n");

    return 0;
}
```

To test the program, you can run it with different duration values and verify that the system clock is indeed suspended for the specified duration. For example:

```
$ ./suspend_clock 5000
Suspending system clock for 5000 milliseconds...
(wait for 5 seconds)
System clock resumed.
```

Note that this example is intended for illustration purposes and is not meant to be a complete solution. In practice, you would need to consider additional factors such as handling invalid input, handling clock resuming in another process, handling system clock changes while the program is running, and handling system clock suspending in another process.

